<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # School Days – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> School Days</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Yesterday Was Wednesday.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Reading: Show comprehension of simple sentences. Reading: Follow written instructions with visual aids. Phonemic Awareness: Recognize all the letters of the alphabet in isolation and the correspondence between letter and sound at the beginning of words.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can show comprehension of the days of the week in simple texts by matching sentences with visual aids (e.g., matching "Yesterday was Wednesday." to a visual that highlights the previous day). Can identify and respond to key words about days in simple texts using visual support (e.g., pointing to "Tomorrow is Friday." in a schedule). Can recognize all the letters of the alphabet and connect each letter to its initial sound in familiar words (e.g., identifying the letter F and its /f/ sound in "Friday" when shown a weekly calendar).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 15px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and displays a large, colorful classroom calendar with the days of the week. The teacher points to 'Today' and asks, "What day is today?" Students respond chorally. Then, the teacher points to 'Yesterday' and asks, "What day was yesterday?" (e.g., Today is Thursday. Yesterday was Wednesday.) This activates prior knowledge of vocabulary (days of the week, yesterday, today, tomorrow) introduced in Lesson 1.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to 'Wednesday' on the calendar and says, "Yesterday was Wednesday." The teacher then points to the day before Wednesday and says, "Tuesday." The teacher models the pronunciation of "Wednesday" (/w/) and "Friday" (/f/), emphasizing the initial sounds. The teacher reads a simple sentence, "Yesterday was Wednesday and it was sunny." while pointing to corresponding visuals.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks, "Was today Wednesday?" (Expected student response: "No, today is Thursday.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks, "Was yesterday Tuesday?" (Expected student response: "No, yesterday was Wednesday.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "School Days")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher displays a large, illustrated weekly school schedule (a simple short notice or a mini-narrative excerpt) on the board or screen. The schedule shows simple sentences about daily activities and highlights specific days. Example sentences: "Yesterday was Wednesday. On Wednesday, we learn Math and read a story. Today is Thursday. Tomorrow is Friday. I like Friday! On Friday, we have English." The text includes pictures for each activity (e.g., books for 'read', a ruler for 'Math', alphabet letters for 'English').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student can understand main ideas and specific details in short, simple written texts supported by visual aids.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> The teacher reads the schedule aloud slowly, pointing to each word. Then asks, "What is this? Is it a story or a school schedule?" Students point to 'school schedule' or say "schedule."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching Sentences to Visuals:</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with sentences from the schedule (e.g., "Yesterday was Wednesday.", "On Friday, we have English.") and separate small pictures (e.g., a calendar with Wednesday highlighted, children in an English class). Students draw a line to match each sentence to its correct picture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Identify Key Words:</b> Students individually circle the words "Wednesday" and "Friday" in the printed schedule text on their worksheet. They also circle the letter 'W' in "Wednesday" and 'F' in "Friday".</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading simple sentences about days of the week, understanding past and present tense with 'be', identifying letters and their initial sounds, and following written instructions. (Grammar: Past simple with "be" - "was"; Present simple - "is"; Vocabulary: days of the week, school subjects, yesterday, today, tomorrow; Pronunciation: /w/, /f/ sounds).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Read and Color:</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with simple sentences and outline drawings. For example: "Yesterday was Wednesday. Color the apple red." (next to a drawing of an apple), "Today is Thursday. Color the sun yellow." (next to a sun drawing). Students read each sentence silently and then follow the instruction by coloring the corresponding picture. This reinforces reading simple commands with visual aids.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice - Missing Word Maze:</b> Students receive a worksheet with a "Days of the Week Maze." The maze has short sentences like "Yesterday ______ Monday." "Today ______ Tuesday." "Tomorrow ______ Wednesday." Students read the sentences and write "was" or "is" in the blanks to complete the path through the maze. They can only move to the next square if they choose the correct word.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Phonemic Awareness - Letter Sound Hunt:</b> The teacher writes sentences on the board: "Yesterday was <b>W</b>ednesday." and "<b>F</b>riday is a fun day!" Students receive individual letter cards (W, F, M, T, S). The teacher asks students to read the sentences on the board silently. Then, the teacher says, "Find the letter for /w/." Students hold up the 'W' card. The teacher then asks, "Find the letter for /f/." Students hold up the 'F' card. The teacher guides them to circle the 'W' and 'F' in the sentences on their personal worksheets and to softly say the initial sound.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-17 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "School Days")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "My Daily Activity Reading Chart"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b> Students receive a "My Daily Activity Reading Chart" template. This chart has boxes for Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday. Below each box, there are 1-2 simple written instructions/sentences. For example:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Monday:</b> Read: "On Monday, we read a book." Draw a book.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Wednesday:</b> Read: "Yesterday was Wednesday. We learned Math." Draw numbers and shapes.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Friday:</b> Read: "Tomorrow is Friday! I like English." Draw letters.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are instructed to read the sentences for each day and then draw a picture in the box that represents the activity described. The teacher circulates, observing students' reading comprehension as they follow the instructions and interpret the text visually. This task focuses on students’ ability to show comprehension of simple sentences by translating written information into visual representations.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on reading comprehension and letter-sound recognition.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task: "Read and Show."</b> The teacher displays a large weekly schedule poster with simple sentences and pictures (similar to Presentation stage but with some new sentences, e.g., "Yesterday was cloudy.", "Today is sunny."). The teacher reads three simple sentences one by one (e.g., "Yesterday was Wednesday.", "Tomorrow is Friday. I like Friday!", "On Wednesday, we have science."). After each sentence, students individually point to the corresponding day on the poster and then point to the correct picture that matches the sentence (e.g., pointing to "Wednesday" and then a picture of a science book). Additionally, the teacher asks students to point to the letter 'W' or 'F' in written words on the poster.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurately points to the correct day of the week based on the simple sentence read.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly matches simple sentences with corresponding visual aids (pictures of activities or weather).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify the letters 'W' and 'F' and demonstrates awareness of their initial sounds by pointing or making the sound.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with faces (happy, neutral, sad) to indicate their understanding.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can read about what day was yesterday. (Circle a face)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can find the letter 'F' in words. (Circle a face)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompts:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What new words did you read today? (e.g., science, schedule)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What part of reading about the days was easy or hard for you?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students successfully matched simple sentences to visual aids, demonstrating comprehension of 'yesterday was', 'today is', and 'tomorrow is'. The text level was appropriate for Pre-A1.4, especially with strong visual support. Reinforcing letter-sound correspondence for /w/ and /f/ was effective during the 'Letter Sound Hunt' activity, but some students needed extra prompting for the initial sound production. I will ensure more varied visual aids are available for individual students who struggled. The 'Read and Color' activity was very engaging and successful for following written instructions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson (Lesson 3 – Speaking & Interaction):</b> "Great job reading about our school days! In our next lesson, we will use these same sentences and words to *speak* about our schedules with our friends, asking and answering questions like 'What is on Monday?' and 'I like Friday!'."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">2-3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>